      "name": "UnwindAndUnlinkListNode",
      "signature": "void UnwindAndUnlinkListNode(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Wrapper function that unwinds and unlinks a list element from its container.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load the list element pointer from the stack (first parameter passed via __stdcall)\n2. Add 4 bytes to the pointer to skip the element header/offset\n3. Delegate to UnlinkListNode to perform the actual node unlinking\n\nParameters:\npListElement: Pointer to the list element to unwind and unlink. The actual\n             node data is offset by +4 bytes from this pointer, suggesting\n             the list element structure has a 4-byte header before the node.\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value. Side effect is unlinking the node from its parent\n       list structure.\n\nSpecial Cases:\nThis is a tail-call optimization thunk that adjusts the pointer parameter\nbefore delegating to UnlinkListNode. The +4 offset adjustment suggests the\nelement structure contains metadata (flags, reference count, etc.) in the\nfirst 4 bytes, with the actual linked list node starting at offset +4.\n\nReferences:\n- UnlinkListNode at 0x6fbf4c20: Performs the actual list node unlinking\n- Called by 40+ functions throughout the codebase for cleanup operations",
